🔒 STIG Remediation: WN10-CC-000280

Title: Remote Desktop Services must always prompt a client for passwords upon connection
Severity: Medium
Status: ✅ Compliant (if properly configured)
Audit Status: Manual Check
STIG ID: WN10-CC-000280
Vuln ID: V-220823
SRG ID: SRG-OS-000373-GPOS-00156
CCI: CCI-002038

🔍 Finding

By default, Remote Desktop Services (RDS) can allow credentials to be saved by the client. This could enable a user to gain unauthorized access without re-authentication. Enforcing the password prompt ensures users must manually authenticate upon each Remote Desktop session, enhancing security and reducing the risk of compromised saved credentials.

🛠️ Manual Configuration

Open Local Group Policy Editor:
Press Win + R, type gpedit.msc, press Enter
Navigate to:

Computer Configuration >>
Administrative Templates >>
Windows Components >>
Remote Desktop Services >>
Remote Desktop Session Host >>
Security

Double-click:
Always prompt for password upon connection
Set the policy to:

Enabled

Click Apply, then OK
✅ Verification (PowerShell)

You can check this policy using PowerShell:
Get-ItemProperty -Path "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Policies\Microsoft\Windows NT\Terminal Services" -Name fPromptForPassword

fPromptForPassword : 1

If the property is missing or set to 0, the setting is not properly configured.

📌 Notes

This setting helps prevent automatic logins using cached credentials on Remote Desktop clients.
If the organization uses smart card login or multi-factor authentication, this setting still applies unless explicitly overridden by another security layer.
Ensure this policy is part of your centralized Group Policy Objects (GPOs) for domain-joined machines if managing at scale.
